-----------------------------------------------------\n1 Introduction (https://web.math.ucsb.edu/~casteels/GeisThesis.pdf)\nciteturn6search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8.3 years ago; At a New York Number Theory Seminar in 2003, Bela Bajnok gave a talk titled *The Spanning Number and the Independence Number of a Subset of an Abelian Group* [2]. Although his focus was on general spanning sets and \\(s\\)-bases for abelian groups, he introduced the concept of a perfect \\(s\\)-basis of size \\(m\\). ... Additionally in [1], Bajnok proves that Conjecture 1.1 holds when \\(s=2,3\\).\n1 Introduction\n\nLet \\(G\\) be a finite abelian group. Given an \\(s \\in \\mathbb{N}\\), a classical problem in additive combinatorics is determining the smallest \\(s\\)-basis for \\(G\\). Formally, finding an \\(m\\) such that\n\n\\[\nm=\\min\\{|A|:\\ A\\subseteq G \\text{ such that } [0,s]A=G\\}.\n\\]\n\nAt a New York Number Theory Seminar in 2003, Bela Bajnok gave a talk titled *The Spanning Number and the Independence Number of a Subset of an Abelian Group* [2]. Although his focus was on general spanning sets and \\(s\\)-bases for abelian groups, he introduced the concept of a perfect \\(s\\)-basis of size \\(m\\). Intuitively, these perfect \\(s\\)-bases are the smallest possible value of \\(m\\) where\n\n\\[\n[0,s]A \\le \\binom{m+s}{s}=|G|.\n\\]\n\nHe notes that he “could not find perfect spanning sets for \\(s \\ge 2\\) and \\(m \\ge 3\\).” Then in his 2017 book titled *Additive Combinatorics: A Menu of Research Problems* [1], he poses the following conjecture:\n\n**Conjecture 1.1.** *There are no perfect \\(s\\)-bases of size \\(m\\) for \\(G\\), unless \\(s=1\\) or \\(m=1\\).*\n\nAdditionally in [1], Bajnok proves that Conjecture 1.1 holds when \\(s=2,3\\).\n\n**Theorem 1.2.** *Conjecture 1.1 holds when \\(s=2,3\\).*\n\nThe main focus of this thesis will be attempting to prove Conjecture 1.1. In particular, we show that Conjecture 1.1 can only hold if \\(s<m\\). Then, we generalize the proof technique for Theorem 1.2 in order to prove more supporting results for the validity of Conjecture 1.1.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIsolation partitions in graphs (https://arxiv.org/abs/2411.03666)\nciteturn6academia16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.0 years ago; Title: Isolation partitions in graphs ... Date: Wed Nov  6 04:52:49 2024 ... In this paper, we prove that every connected graph with maximum degree at most $k$, except $k$-clique, can be partitioned into $k+1$ disjoint $k$-clique isolating sets, and that every connected claw-free subcubic graph, except 3-cycle, can be partitioned into four disjoint cycle isolating sets.\nTitle: Isolation partitions in graphs\nAuthors: Gang Zhang, Weiling Yang, Xian'an Jin\nDate: Wed Nov  6 04:52:49 2024\n\nLet $G$ be a graph and $k \\geq 3$ an integer. A subset $D \\subseteq V(G)$ is a $k$-clique (resp., cycle) isolating set of $G$ if $G-N[D]$ contains no $k$-clique (resp., cycle). In this paper, we prove that every connected graph with maximum degree at most $k$, except $k$-clique, can be partitioned into $k+1$ disjoint $k$-clique isolating sets, and that every connected claw-free subcubic graph, except 3-cycle, can be partitioned into four disjoint cycle isolating sets. As a consequence of the first result, every $k$-regular graph can be partitioned into $k+1$ disjoint $k$-clique isolating sets.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAntimagic Labeling of Symmetric Digraphs | PDF | Vertex (Graph Theory) | Graph Theory (https://ro.scribd.com/document/288466001/Further-results-on-Antimagic-Digraphs)\nciteturn6search1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 11.0 years ago; The corresponding antimagic labeling of the symmetric digraph ... w(v j ) 3r s 2 2 j , 1 j s,\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIsolation of k-cliques - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/arti